// Title: Bypass UAC via Fodhelper.exe
// Sigma ID: 7f741dcf-fc22-4759-87b4-9ae8376676a2
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: E.M. Anhaus (originally from Atomic Blue Detections, Tony Lambert), oscd.community
// Date: 2019-10-24 | Modified: 2021-11-27
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, T1548.002
// Description: Identifies use of Fodhelper.exe to bypass User Account Control. Adversaries use this technique
//   to execute privileged processes.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use of fodhelper.exe utility by legitimate user
// References:
//   - https://eqllib.readthedocs.io/en/latest/analytics/e491ce22-792f-11e9-8f5c-d46d6d62a49e.html
//   - https://github.com/redcanaryco/atomic-red-team/blob/f339e7da7d05f6057fdfcdd3742bfcf365fee2a9/atomics/T1548.002/T1548.002.md
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_uac_bypass_fodhelper.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceProcessEvents
| where InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\fodhelper.exe"
